1. Install MISP on a VM
(Install MISP on a separate VM. Running Wazuh, MISP, and Caldera on the same VM can overload resources.)
Download MISP: https://www.misp-project.org/
Import the VM into VMware/VirtualBox OR install manually on Ubuntu 24.04 following the official guide: MISP Installation Guide

2. Configure MISP
Start the MISP VM and log in to the web interface: https://<MISP-IP>.
Create an admin user if necessary.
Create a test event:
Event Name: Windows-Test-Threat
Threat Type: Malware / Other
Attribute: IP address of the Windows 10 agent
Save the event.
Copy the API key(auth key) of your MISP user — you will need this for Wazuh integration.

3. Configure Wazuh to Fetch MISP Events
Edit Wazuh manager configuration:
sudo nano /var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf
Add the MISP Wodle section (replace <MISP-IP> and <MISP-API-KEY> with your actual values):
<wodle name="misp">
<disabled>no</disabled>
<interval>1m</interval>
<url>https://<MISP-IP></url>
<key><MISP-API-KEY></key>
<verifycert>no</verifycert>
</wodle>
Save the file and restart Wazuh:
sudo systemctl restart wazuh-manager

4. Verify Event Fetching
Check Wazuh logs:
sudo tail -f /var/ossec/logs/ossec.log
You should see lines similar to:
misp: Event <ID> fetched successfully

5. Test Detection
Simulate activity from the Windows agent (e.g., ping, scripts, or monitored actions).
Wazuh should generate an alert for the IP fetched from MISP.
Verify in the Wazuh Dashboard → Alerts.
